        "day_number": 95,
        "title": "April 4",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“For he has made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Corinthians 5:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Scripture's call to repentance is an invitation to truth and restored life, not permission to shame or terrorize. Name actual wrongdoing, protect anyone harmed, receive Christ's mercy, and take a concrete step toward repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy and merciful God, give me courage to confess truthfully, receive forgiveness, and repair what I can. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Come you, and let us go up to the mountain of the Lord.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 2:3</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 5:21",
          "Isaiah 2:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 96,
        "title": "April 5",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“On him they laid the cross, that he might bear it after Jesus.”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 23:26</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> The cross reveals Christ's self-giving love and God's refusal to abandon a wounded world. Receive forgiveness as grace, name harm truthfully, and let reconciliation include accountability and repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Crucified and risen Lord, forgive me, free me from sin, and make your self-giving love visible in my life. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Before honour is humility.”</em></p>\n<p>Proverbs 15:33</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Wisdom listens before speaking, tests appearances, welcomes correction, and seeks the good of others. Ask what is true, what love requires, and whose experience needs to be heard before you act.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, give me a teachable heart, truthful speech, and discernment that produces peace and justice. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 23:26",
          "Proverbs 15:33"
        ]
      },
