      {
        "day_number": 98,
        "title": "April 7",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“You sons of men, how long will you turn my glory into shame?”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 4:2</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Spurgeon describes the mockery surrounding Jesus' crucifixion, but his original wording assigns blame to the people of Israel as a whole. The Gospel accounts describe particular Roman soldiers, leaders, and crowds acting in a complex historical setting; they do not justify blaming Jewish people collectively. Read Psalm 4:2 alongside Luke 23:33–43. Jesus bore public humiliation and answered with mercy. What would it look like to defend someone whose dignity is being mocked today? The inscription at the cross named Jesus as King of the Jews (Luke 23:38). Some onlookers rejected him, while others followed or mourned him; no entire people should be identified with the decision to crucify him. Jesus met hostility without returning contempt. Ask God to make your speech truthful and protective of the dignity of those who worship differently.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Deliver me from bloodguiltiness, O God, you God of my salvation; and my tongue shall sing aloud of your righteousness.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 51:14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Psalm 51:14 is a prayer for mercy after grave wrongdoing. David's responsibility for Uriah's death cannot be explained away. Confession names the harm honestly, seeks forgiveness, and calls for repair where repair is possible. If you have hurt someone, avoid excuses; seek wise help, protect those harmed, and take a concrete step toward accountability. God's mercy never makes another person's suffering insignificant.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 4:2",
          "Psalm 51:14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 99,
        "title": "April 8",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“If they do these things in a green tree, what shall be done in the dry?”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 23:31</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“I will fear no evil: for you are with me.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 23:4</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> This verse meets human fear, grief, illness, or weakness without turning suffering into proof of guilt. Trust in God can coexist with lament, safety planning, medical care, counseling, and the support of a faithful community.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, stay near in suffering, lead me toward wise help, and make me attentive to another person's pain. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 23:31",
          "Psalm 23:4"
        ]
      },
