      {
        "day_number": 125,
        "title": "May 4",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Shall a man make gods to himself, and they are no gods.”</em></p>\n<p>Jeremiah 16:20</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Jeremiah 16:20 challenges the worship of human-made gods. Spurgeon extends that warning to competing loyalties, but the original passage speculates harshly about parents who love their children and suggests bereavement could result from such love. That claim is omitted. Loving and protecting children is a good responsibility, not a reason to fear divine punishment. Ask whether possessions, status, or control have become more important to you than love of God and neighbor. Practice generous care for someone who depends on you.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Being born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible.”</em></p>\n<p>1 Peter 1:23</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Holiness is life being reshaped by communion with God. It appears in integrity, compassion, self-control, justice, and love rather than contempt for people who struggle or confidence in our own superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Jeremiah 16:20",
          "1 Peter 1:23"
        ]
      },
