        "day_number": 139,
        "title": "May 18",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“In him dwells all the fulness of the Godhead bodily. And you are complete in him.”</em></p>\n<p>Colossians 2:9,10</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Holiness is life being reshaped by communion with God. It appears in integrity, compassion, self-control, justice, and love rather than contempt for people who struggle or confidence in our own superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Afterward.”</em></p>\n<p>Hebrews 12:11</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Hebrews 12:11 acknowledges that discipline is painful before it can produce growth. Spurgeon uses this image to assure readers that hardship will yield good, but neither this verse nor faithful pastoral care requires us to call every injury a blessing. When suffering comes, grief and protest can be honest prayers. Seek treatment, safety, companionship, and practical assistance as needed. Ask what wisdom or kindness may become possible, while refusing to minimize pain or blame someone for experiencing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Colossians 2:9,10",
          "Hebrews 12:11"
        ]
      },
