      {
        "day_number": 148,
        "title": "May 27",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“So Mephibosheth dwelt in Jerusalem: for he did eat continually at the king’s table; and was lame on both his feet.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Samuel 9:13</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> David welcomes Mephibosheth to the royal table because of his covenant with Jonathan. Spurgeon's original reading describes Mephibosheth's disabled body as a deformity and then uses mobility impairment as a symbol of weak faith and sin; that ableist analogy is omitted. Mephibosheth belongs at the table without needing to become nondisabled. In Christ, people of every body and ability share equal dignity. Remove a barrier, listen to disabled people, and make your table and worship genuinely accessible.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“What is your servant, that you should look upon such a dead dog as I am?”</em></p>\n<p>2 Samuel 9:8</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Mephibosheth uses the self-deprecating expression 'dead dog' before King David. Spurgeon's original application intensifies that image into self-hatred, contempt for human bodies, and insulting comparisons with animals; those elements are omitted. Humility means telling the truth about our dependence and wrongdoing, not denying the worth God gives us or degrading other creatures. Receive grace with gratitude, confess without self-abuse, and honor the image of God in yourself and your neighbor.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Samuel 9:13",
          "2 Samuel 9:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 149,
        "title": "May 28",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Whom he justified, them he also glorified.”</em></p>\n<p>Romans 8:30</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Holiness is life being reshaped by communion with God. It appears in integrity, compassion, self-control, justice, and love rather than contempt for people who struggle or confidence in our own superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“This I recall to my mind, therefore have I hope.”</em></p>\n<p>Lamentations 3:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Romans 8:30",
          "Lamentations 3:21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 150,
        "title": "May 29",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“You hate wickedness.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 45:7</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Worship turns attention toward the triune God and reorders desire around truth, beauty, justice, and love. Praise need not hide grief; thanksgiving and lament can both be faithful speech before God.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Cursed be the man before the Lord, that rises up and builds this city Jericho.”</em></p>\n<p>Joshua 6:26</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Joshua 6:26 concerns ancient Jericho. It does not authorize Christians to curse or demean other churches. Spurgeon applied the verse to a nineteenth-century dispute, and that polemical application is omitted here. Ask the Lord for discernment, humility, and truthful speech that honors the people with whom you disagree.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 45:7",
          "Joshua 6:26"
        ]
      },
