        "day_number": 227,
        "title": "August 14",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“You, Lord, have made me glad through your work.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 92:4</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Psalm 92 rejoices in God's work, and the gospel announces forgiveness through Christ. Spurgeon's original reading quotes a willingness to receive sickness, poverty, loss, and persecution as though pardon made such harms unimportant; that conclusion is omitted. Forgiveness can give hope within suffering, but it does not require anyone to welcome abuse, neglect health, or minimize grief. Give thanks for grace, seek safety and care when needed, and let joy become practical solidarity with people who suffer.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Generous God, help me serve with humility, respect my real limits, and share what I have for the common good. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“I know their sorrows.”</em></p>\n<p>Exodus 3:7</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> In Exodus 3, God says that he has seen oppression, heard the people's cry, and knows their suffering; God's knowledge leads toward deliverance. Spurgeon's original reflection tells sufferers not to analyze symptoms or question what is happening, using physician, master, and body metaphors to discourage inquiry; that counsel is omitted. Trust can coexist with questions, lament, medical investigation, and action against injustice. Tell God and trustworthy helpers what hurts, seek competent care, and listen seriously when another person describes pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 92:4",
          "Exodus 3:7"
        ]
      },
