        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“God is jealous.”</em></p>\n<p>Nahum 1:2</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“I will sing of mercy and judgment.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 101:1</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> God's mercy is gift, not payment for achievement or respectability. Receive grace without self-contempt, extend it without favoritism, and remember that forgiveness never makes another person's suffering unimportant.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, let your grace free me from shame and pride, and make me generous in forgiveness, truth, and repair. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Nahum 1:2",
          "Psalm 101:1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 257,
        "title": "September 13",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Who passing through the valley of Baca make it a well, the rain also fills the pools.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 84:6</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Creation is God's good gift, entrusted to human care rather than exploitation. Receive its beauty with gratitude, respect the limits of creatures and ecosystems, and practice stewardship that serves future neighbors too.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Creator of heaven and earth, awaken gratitude and teach me to care faithfully for the world and its creatures. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“This man receives sinners.”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 15:2</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 84:6",
          "Luke 15:2"
        ]
      },
