        "day_number": 258,
        "title": "September 14",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“There were also with him other little ships.”</em></p>\n<p>Mark 4:36</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“I acknowledged my sin to you, and mine iniquity have I not hid. I said, I will confess my transgressions to the Lord; and you forgave the iniquity of my sin.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 32:5</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Scripture's call to repentance is an invitation to truth and restored life, not permission to shame or terrorize. Name actual wrongdoing, protect anyone harmed, receive Christ's mercy, and take a concrete step toward repair.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy and merciful God, give me courage to confess truthfully, receive forgiveness, and repair what I can. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 4:36",
          "Psalm 32:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 259,
        "title": "September 15",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“He shall not be afraid of evil tidings.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 112:7</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse meets human fear, grief, illness, or weakness without turning suffering into proof of guilt. Trust in God can coexist with lament, safety planning, medical care, counseling, and the support of a faithful community.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, stay near in suffering, lead me toward wise help, and make me attentive to another person's pain. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“A people near to him.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 148:14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Worship turns attention toward the triune God and reorders desire around truth, beauty, justice, and love. Praise need not hide grief; thanksgiving and lament can both be faithful speech before God.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 112:7",
          "Psalm 148:14"
        ]
      },
