        "day_number": 264,
        "title": "September 20",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The sword of the Lord, and of Gideon.”</em></p>\n<p>Judges 7:20</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Judges 7 recounts Gideon's actions within an ancient conflict. Spurgeon's original application turns its weapons and battle cry into an image for forceful evangelism, including language about not allowing others to escape the message; that coercive framing is omitted. Christians bear witness through truthful speech, visible love, patient service, and respect for conscience. Let your light shine by doing one good work without pressure or manipulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“In the evening withhold not your hand.”</em></p>\n<p>Ecclesiastes 11:6</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Ecclesiastes 11:6 encourages patient faithfulness when results are uncertain. Spurgeon's original reading burdens readers with responsibility for other people's eternal fate and describes later life mainly as decay; those claims are omitted. Faithful service can include prayer, work, rest, mentoring, and receiving help. Ask what love requires today, and honor the gifts and dignity of people at every age.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, give me a teachable heart, truthful speech, and discernment that produces peace and justice. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Judges 7:20",
          "Ecclesiastes 11:6"
        ]
      },
