      {
        "day_number": 329,
        "title": "November 24",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The glorious Lord will be to us a place of broad rivers and streams.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 33:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Isaiah 33:21 pictures the Lord as a place of broad rivers, security, and abundance. Spurgeon's original reading says that any unmet need is the sufferer's own fault; that claim is omitted. God's presence does not erase material need or justify blaming people who lack food, housing, care, or safety. Receive God's refuge, name your needs honestly, and help the church become a place where resources and burdens are shared.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Yet a little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands to sleep: so shall your poverty come as one that travels; and your want as an armed man.”</em></p>\n<p>Proverbs 24:33,34</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Proverbs 24:33–34 warns that repeated neglect can overtake a field and household. It does not teach that every experience of poverty comes from laziness, nor does it require terror about a missed spiritual deadline. Economic hardship has many causes, and healthy rest is not moral failure. Consider one responsibility you have postponed, take a manageable step, and extend practical compassion rather than judgment to people under strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, give me a teachable heart, truthful speech, and discernment that produces peace and justice. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 33:21",
          "Proverbs 24:33,34"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 330,
        "title": "November 25",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“To preach deliverance to the captives.”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 4:18</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“For he says to Moses, I will have mercy on whom I will have mercy, and I will have compassion on whom I will have compassion.”</em></p>\n<p>Romans 9:15</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> God's mercy is gift, not payment for achievement or respectability. Receive grace without self-contempt, extend it without favoritism, and remember that forgiveness never makes another person's suffering unimportant.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Merciful God, let your grace free me from shame and pride, and make me generous in forgiveness, truth, and repair. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 4:18",
          "Romans 9:15"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 331,
        "title": "November 26",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might.”</em></p>\n<p>Ecclesiastes 9:10</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Wisdom listens before speaking, tests appearances, welcomes correction, and seeks the good of others. Ask what is true, what love requires, and whose experience needs to be heard before you act.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, give me a teachable heart, truthful speech, and discernment that produces peace and justice. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“They shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel.”</em></p>\n<p>Zechariah 4:10</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Worship turns attention toward the triune God and reorders desire around truth, beauty, justice, and love. Praise need not hide grief; thanksgiving and lament can both be faithful speech before God.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ecclesiastes 9:10",
          "Zechariah 4:10"
        ]
      },
