      {
        "day_number": 359,
        "title": "December 24",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“For your sakes he became poor.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Corinthians 8:9</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> God's concern for people who are poor, oppressed, excluded, or unheard calls for more than private sympathy. Notice whose dignity or safety is at stake, listen before acting, and join mercy to practical justice.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of justice and compassion, show me where love requires action, and give me courage to protect dignity and seek the good of my neighbor. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“The glory of the Lord shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see it together.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 40:5</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Isaiah 40:5 promises that the Lord's glory will be revealed for all people to see. Christians can share that hope openly while honoring the dignity of neighbors who believe differently. Pray for faithful witness, patient listening, and a world in which justice and mercy are visible.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Corinthians 8:9",
          "Isaiah 40:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 360,
        "title": "December 25",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“See, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 7:14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Isaiah 7:14 became for Christians a sign pointing to Jesus, Immanuel, truly God and truly human. Spurgeon's original Christmas reflection assigns the entrance of sin chiefly to a woman before contrasting Eve with Mary; that degrading gender claim is omitted. Scripture holds all humanity responsible for sin and presents Mary as responding faithfully to God's call. Worship Christ, and honor the equal dignity and vocation of women and men in his body.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“And it was so, when the days of their feasting were gone about, that Job sent and sanctified them, and rose up early in the morning, and offered burnt offerings according to the number of them all: for Job said, It may be that my sons have sinned, and cursed God in their hearts. Thus did Job continually.”</em></p>\n<p>Job 1:5</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> This verse meets human fear, grief, illness, or weakness without turning suffering into proof of guilt. Trust in God can coexist with lament, safety planning, medical care, counseling, and the support of a faithful community.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, stay near in suffering, lead me toward wise help, and make me attentive to another person's pain. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 7:14",
          "Job 1:5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 361,
