        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> John 11:43-45; John 12:9-11</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Sherlock’s defender responds to claims about the sealed tomb and a theory that the raising of Lazarus was a known fraud. He asks whether there is evidence of that accusation and distinguishes an allegation from facts reported in the Gospels.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> John records Lazarus leaving the tomb and shows how differently people responded: some believed, while others opposed Jesus. A reaction to an event, whether enthusiastic or suspicious, is not the same as an independent investigation. As we read, we can be open to what the Gospel intends to proclaim while identifying where arguments about its historicity rely on interpretation.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Do I assume an accusation becomes evidence merely because it has been repeated?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Before passing on a serious allegation, identify its original source and what has actually been established.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, protect my speech from rumor and give me a love for careful truth. Amen.</p>",
