        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Matthew 28:11-15; Proverbs 18:13-17</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> In this part of Sherlock’s dialogue, the defense addresses the reported story that disciples stole Jesus’ body. He examines whether people who were asleep could know what happened and argues over what testimony can fairly establish.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Matthew tells a story about a competing explanation for the empty tomb. Proverbs warns that answering before hearing is folly. Both readings challenge us to distinguish what a witness saw from what a witness inferred or heard from someone else. Fairness does not demand treating every account as equally strong; it asks us to understand why we trust one explanation and question another.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Am I willing to identify the limits of my own knowledge when I testify about something?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> For one disputed story, label each statement firsthand observation, secondhand report, or inference.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Wise God, make me honest about what I know and generous when others struggle to understand. Amen.</p>",
