        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Matthew 28:1-15; Proverbs 14:15</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> Sherlock’s defender presses the practical difficulties of the body-theft explanation: the disciples’ fear, the presence of guards, and what would be required to move a body unnoticed. These remain arguments developed from the Gospel narrative rather than independently verified reconstructions.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Proverbs praises the prudent person who weighs a step rather than believing every report. Skepticism can be useful, but it is incomplete when it invents a suspicious story without checking how it might have occurred. Likewise, believing the resurrection need not mean refusing difficult questions. Careful thinking follows a claim through its consequences and recognizes its limits.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Do I find a theory persuasive because it sounds clever, or because it fits the evidence available?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Pick one explanation of a difficult event. Ask what facts would make it stronger, and what would count against it.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, give me patience with complex questions and freedom from careless suspicion. Amen.</p>",
