        "title": "The Resurrection Matters to the Whole Faith",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> 1 Corinthians 15:12-20; Luke 24:36-43</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> A new section of the staged trial asks whether reports of Jesus alive after death can bear the weight placed upon them. The skeptic argues that so extraordinary an event cannot be established by ordinary testimony and questions the character of the appearances.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> Paul takes resurrection seriously enough to say that Christian hope would be empty without it. Luke describes startled disciples receiving reassurance from Jesus. These texts neither forbid honest doubt nor pretend every question can be solved by confidence alone. They show why the claim matters: Easter is presented as more than a metaphor for positive thinking.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> What difference would it make to my faith if resurrection were only a symbol?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read 1 Corinthians 15:12–20. Write down the specific consequences Paul connects with Christ’s rising.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Risen Lord, help me face the central claims of my faith with openness and care. Amen.</p>",
