        "title": "Trace the Story from Claim to Conclusion",
        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 23:44-56; Matthew 27:62-66</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The mock judge now summarizes the entire case. He divides it into three periods: Jesus’ ministry, the interval from his death to the reported resurrection, and the later testimony. This segment especially revisits the preparation of the alleged fraud and the sealed tomb.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> A complicated question becomes easier to assess when we identify which stage each statement concerns. Luke tells of Jesus’ death and burial; Matthew adds the guard story. The accounts were written for theological purposes as well as narration. Respectful reading requires us not to mix separate moments or claim an ancient narrator answered questions he did not address.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Can I explain the difference between the event being claimed and the later interpretation offered?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Draw a simple timeline of the passages, labeling narrated events separately from inferences made by later interpreters.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord, grant me patience for careful reading and freedom from rushed conclusions. Amen.</p>",
