        "content": "<p><strong>Read:</strong> Luke 24:36-49; John 20:24-29</p><p><strong>Source and historical context:</strong> The judge recaps arguments about Jesus’ bodily appearances, Mary Magdalene, the disciples behind closed doors, the selection of witnesses, and the women’s testimony. He also acknowledges the skeptic’s warning that suffering for a belief does not by itself establish that belief.</p><p><strong>Devotional focus:</strong> The Gospels portray people surprised, afraid, questioning, and slowly convinced. These human responses matter more than the caricature that faith always arrives without thought. A fair review does not suppress difficult evidence or mock doubts. It asks what the sources say and what follows reasonably from them, while remembering that real people live behind our arguments.</p><p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Can I describe a sincere objection to my belief without mocking it?</p><p><strong>Practice:</strong> Summarize a viewpoint you disagree with in three fair sentences. Then read John 20:24–29 with empathy for Thomas.</p><p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus, make me teachable and respectful while I look for truth. Amen.</p>",
